3810,http://neurovault.org/collections/3810/,http://neurovault.org/collections/3810/download,2538,,MartineGroefsema,15,"Brain responses to anticipating and receiving beer: Comparing light, at‐risk, and dependent alcohol users",,10.1111/adb.12766,,"Martine M. Groefsema, Rutger C.M.E. Engels, Valerie Voon, Arnt F.A. Schellekens, Maartje Luijten and Guillaume Sescousse",https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/adb.12766,Addiction Biology,,,False,2018-05-07T18:10:52.270568+02:00,2019-05-08T23:26:44.151587+02:00,2019-05-08T23:26:44.147942+02:00,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,[]
1324,http://neurovault.org/collections/1324/,http://neurovault.org/collections/1324/download,555,,ANIMA,30,"The Right Dorsal Premotor Mosaic: Organization, Functions, and Connectivity",,10.1093/cercor/bhw065,,"Sarah Genon, Hai Li, Lingzhong Fan, Veronika I. Müller, Edna C. Cieslik, Felix Hoffstaedter, Andrew T. Reid, Robert Langner, Christian Grefkes, Peter T. Fox, Susanne Moebus, Svenja Caspers, Katrin Amunts, Tianzi Jiang and Simon B. Eickhoff",http://www.cercor.oxfordjournals.org/lookup/doi/10.1093/cercor/bhw065,Cerebral Cortex,"We identified five subregions within the right dorsal premotor cortex by using connectivity-based parcellation. We functionally characterized them in terms of functional connectivity and associated behavioral functions. This highlighted a cognitivo-motor gradient along the three subregions on the rostro-caudal axis: rostral, central and caudal. Furthermore, we identified a ventral subregion showing premotor eye-field functions and a dorsal one preferentially related to finder/hand movements. This dataset was automatically imported from the ANIMA <http://anima.modelgui.org/> database. Version: 2",http://anima.fz-juelich.de/api/studies/Genon_CBPrightPMd_2016,False,2016-04-24T21:48:03.618822+02:00,2018-06-30T06:22:17.800331+02:00,2016-04-24T21:48:03.618192+02:00,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,[]
425,http://neurovault.org/collections/425/,http://neurovault.org/collections/425/download,186,"ChrisFiloGorgolewski, RussPoldrack",vsochat,37,Prefrontal-Subcortical Pathways Mediating Successful Emotion Regulation,,10.1016/j.neuron.2008.09.006,,"Tor D. Wager, Matthew L. Davidson, Brent L. Hughes, Martin A. Lindquist and Kevin N. Ochsner",http://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0896627308007538,Neuron,"OpenfMRI ds000108. Although prefrontal cortex has been implicated in the cognitive regulation of emotion, the cortical-subcortical interactions that mediate this ability remain poorly understood. To address this issue, we identified a right ventrolateral prefrontal region (vlPFC) whose activity correlated with reduced negative emotional experience during cognitive reappraisal of aversive images. We then applied a pathway-mapping analysis on subcortical regions to locate mediators of the association between vlPFC activity and reappraisal success (i.e., reductions in reported emotion). Results identified two separable pathways that together explained approximately 50% of the reported variance in self-reported emotion: (1) a path through nucleus accumbens that predicted greater reappraisal success, and (2) a path through ventral amygdala that predicted reduced reappraisal success (i.e., more negative emotion). These results provide direct evidence that vlPFC is involved in both the generation and regulation of emotion through different subcortical pathways, suggesting a general role for this region in appraisal processes.",https://openfmri.org/dataset/ds000108,False,2015-03-06T02:22:46.836271+01:00,2018-06-30T07:01:16.123763+02:00,2015-03-06T02:22:46.836271+01:00,,,,,,,,,22.18,18.0,41.0,,0.5,,,,,GE,Signa Twin Speed Excite HD scanner,1.5,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,[]
