20036,http://neurovault.org/collections/20036/,http://neurovault.org/collections/20036/download,6545,,hongkn1,4,Neural subnetwork signatures distinguishing source and item memory retrieval: A meta-analysis of 66 fMRI studies,published,10.1162/imag.a.124,,Hongkeun Kim,https://direct.mit.edu/imag/article/doi/10.1162/IMAG.a.124/132197/Neural-subnetwork-signatures-distinguishing-source,Imaging Neuroscience,"This study investigated the intrinsic brain networks underlying source and item memory retrieval through a meta-analysis of 66 fMRI studies, with a focus on subnetwork-level distinctions within major intrinsic networks. Three key findings emerged. First, within the Frontoparietal Network, source memory retrieval elicited markedly stronger engagement of Subnetwork A than item memory retrieval, suggesting heightened demands on controlled processing during complex retrieval; Subnetworks B and C showed minimal or no differences. Second, within the Default Mode Network, item memory retrieval—but not source memory retrieval—significantly engaged Subnetworks A and B, consistent with a more vivid or coherent subjective recollection experience during simpler tasks; Subnetwork C showed no significant involvement. Third, within the Ventral Attention–Salience Network, source memory retrieval selectively recruited Subnetwork B more than item memory retrieval, possibly reflecting increased demands on response coordination; Subnetwork A showed no significant differences. These findings offer new insights into the distinct neural mechanisms supporting different forms of episodic memory retrieval and highlight the value of subnetwork-level analyses in isolating the dynamic processes that support memory.",,False,2025-03-28T01:46:53.831040+01:00,2026-03-16T06:15:50.059432+01:00,2025-10-19T03:31:32.982338+02:00,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,[]
